fix: reject program-launcher escapes (open, dot/source) in the shell sandbox

The layer-3 program analysis classified interpreters, build runners, pagers and
network launchers, but not process/document launchers. A command the sandbox
called safe could therefore still execute arbitrary code:

    open -a Terminal /tmp/x.sh        → Terminal runs the script
    open -b com.apple.Terminal /tmp/x.sh → same, selected by bundle id
    open /tmp/x.command               → LaunchServices runs the .command file
    . /tmp/x.sh                       → the POSIX source builtin evaluates it

macOS `open` is a program launcher and Zia already has dedicated, verified
open_app/open_browser tools for legitimate launching, so run_shell never needs
it; the `.`/source builtin is an alias of the already-blocked `source`. Both are
now rejected, while `.` used as an ordinary argument (find . -name …) stays
permitted.

Adds a focused regression suite and strengthens the release-gate sandbox checks.

